On Sun, 7 Jun 2026 at 23:28, Mike Taylor <sauropoda@gmail.com<mailto:sauropoda@gmail.com>> wrote:
Hi, Matt and Abby. As I push on with the AMNH Barosaurus paper, I have noticed an interesting anomaly. I'll quote from the in-progress manuscript:

Brown‚Äôs (1929) memorandum on the trading he did for fossils has a rather different list of Barosaurus material at the USNM than that which Gilmore (1923b:3) specified when sending the fossils there. Where Gilmore had listed 13 cervicals, three dorsals, right scapula, humerus and radius and a sternal plate, Brown listed 10 cervicals, three anterior dorsals, left scapula and humerus. These discrepancies are significant. The difference in the number of cervicals can be explained as a consequence of supposed pairs turning out to be single long vertebrae, but the left/right differences for the appendicular material are harder to account for. The radius and sternal plate mentioned by Gilmore seem to have gone missing: they are not included in the material list below. It is possible that they still lurk unrecognised in the USNM collections.

The references are below, and I have attached PDFs of both.

What do you think? IS is possible? Is there a way to find out?

Many thanks,

-- Mike.


References

Brown, 1929. Report on Barosaurus (letter to the American Museum of Natural History, 31 December 1929. Reproduced on page 21 in: Dingus, Lowell. 1996. Next of Kin: Great Fossils at the American Museum of Natural History. Rizzoli, New York.

Gilmore, Charles W. 1923b. Letter to William de Chastignier Ravenel, ‚ÄúI herewith submit my report on the expedition to the Dinosaur National Monument‚Äù. 30 August 1923. 3 pages.


